The Shepherd: It’s alright, my dear~. No need to rush. If you are hiding, then let the time come when you feel ready. We are here to welcome you, always!

Follower of The Shepherd: Our Shepherd’s grace… such understanding…

The Shepherd: Huhu~ 
The Shepherd: But tell me… what brings you here? How did you find out about this place? 

FIN1X: I heard about you from the farmer.

The Shepherd: …farmer?

FIN1X: Yes, the hybrid who lives in the city.

The Shepherd: Ah… I see. 
The Shepherd: I’m sorry you had to encounter that poor creature.

FIN1X: Poor Creature?

The Shepherd: Of course… you wouldn’t understand. 
The Shepherd: You’re one of them, after all. 

FIN1X: ..!

The Shepherd: Surely, you knew I’d notice eventually. 
Huhu.

FIN1X: …

The Shepherd: The stench of rust and metal gave you away long before you got here. So this is humanity’s masterpiece? Pitiful. No matter how many times I see it, the sight never ceases to disgust me. 

The Shepherd: Long ago, before the war, beings like that were made to play god.
Now, even after all that’s passed, such beings cling to the illusion of control–of life.
The Shepherd: My people and I… we believe nature is reclaiming what was always hers.
The Shepherd: That creature and you… It is no man, nor machine, nor beast.
The Shepherd: It is something that defies nature–it should not exist. 

You: And who decides what should or should not exist?

The Shepherd: He is a reminder of a broken age. A living memory of mankind’s failure.
The Shepherd: No matter what he claims, he cannot change what he is. Neither can you.

FIN1X: I was created to preserve what was left of humanity… to help. If I am flawed, it is not by choice. I do not understand your hostility–and I do not wish to harm you. 

The Shepherd: Save humanity? Those pitiful creatures, the so-called ‘evolution’ of apes? It’s clear you can’t see beyond your programming if you believe I’d ever let those parasites return and ravage what little remains of our Mother Earth. 


FIN1X: I may be a cyborg… a machine, but I understand the value of life–whether it’s human, animal, or nature itself. Humanity’s flaws don’t define them entirely. There’s still hope, and I believe there’s a way for all to coexist if given the chance. 

The Shepherd: Coexist? You don’t understand. They had their chance, and they squandered it. Look around you–this world is all that’s left. The Earth is finally beginning to heal without them, and you want to undo that?

FIN1X: Humanity made mistakes, but they also created beauty–creations worth preserving– 

The Shepherd: No! You don’t know the full story…
The Shepherd: The humans... destroying everything in their wake and driving themselves to the brink of extinction. My father, along with a few others, was fortunate enough to survive. He was chosen by Mother Nature herself and sent on a space expedition before it all fell apart.
The Shepherd: When he returned to Earth, he knew he was meant to lead the rebirth of this planet. That war – it was no accident. It was a divine cleansing, a necessary purging. With the human plague wiped out, Earth has flourished. It has never been more pure, more alive.
The Shepherd: Now, I take up my father’s will and continue his mission–to protect this new world from repeating the mistakes of the old. 


FIN1X: Your father’s worldview is built on delusion. You speak with certainty, but you were not there. You did not experience humanity before the war. Without firsthand knowledge, your conclusions are based solely on inherited perspective. How can you claim to understand what they were–or what they might have become? 

The Shepherd: …

FIN1X: You claim to protect what’s left, yet you disregard the lives right in front of you.

The Shepherd: …
 
FIN1X: The farmer chooses to stay and grow life.
FIN1X: Maybe you both are not so different.

The Shepherd: Don’t compare me with a machine.

FIN1X: …
FIN1X: I ask of you to let the farmer go.

The Shepherd: …Fine. You will not change my mind, but continuing this argument over the farmer’s life is pointless. I will spare him, and spare you for now, as the first to challenge my resolve. 
The Shepherd: Leave my land now, before it’s too late.

FIN1X: <i>It seems like I’ve persuaded the human enough to keep my promise to protect the farmer.</i> 
